# Backend

## Files

- One class / interface / record / enum per file, named after the type.
- Interface and implementation live in separate files.
- Partial classes are fine for splitting a large type (e.g. `BookingService.Credits.cs`).
- Small request/response DTOs used by a single endpoint may share a file.
- Split existing multi-type files only when a slice already touches them.

## Migrations

- Every schema change gets an EF migration.
- New columns are nullable or have a default. `Down()` must work.
- SQL is idempotent and safe on SQL Server.
- Never edit a migration that is already merged; add a new one.
- Clean up duplicate rows before adding a unique index.
- Data-only updates use `WHERE` on the old value so they are safe to re-run.

## Money

- Amounts are paise as `long` or `decimal`, never `double`.
- Webhooks and jobs are safe to run twice (idempotent).
- Reconciliation only reports differences. It never fixes money itself.

## Time

- Store UTC. Inject `TimeProvider`; never call `DateTime.Now`/`UtcNow` in business code.
- Convert to IST only for business-day rules and display.

## EF Core

- Async calls with a `CancellationToken`. `AsNoTracking()` for reads.
- Admin lists page and filter in SQL, never in memory.

## Jobs

- Recurring work runs on `RecurringJobScheduler`, with its schedule in `PlatformSettings`.

## Logging (from 3.5.10)

- Use `ILogger` (Serilog underneath). No `Console.Write`.
- The correlation id is in the log scope, the `X-Correlation-Id` response header, `ApiErrorResponses` bodies and `ErrorEvent`.
- Never log OTP codes, full phone numbers, email addresses, JWTs, passwords, Razorpay secrets or card data. Use the shared `LogMask` helper.
- Never log request/response bodies or `Authorization` / `Cookie` headers.
- Masking also applies to data stored in `ErrorEvent` and to stored webhook payloads.
- Global error handler: no stack traces in responses. 5xx writes an `ErrorEvent`; 4xx does not.
- `ErrorEvent` rows are deleted by the clean-up job after the retention days in `PlatformSettings`.

## Security

- Every new admin endpoint is admin-only.
- Every read or change of user data checks that the caller owns it (or is admin).
